\title{BiSCaL: bivariate separable causal effects in longitudinal studies}
\description{
Causal inference for two ordinal (including binary) responses measured
repeatedly over time, for example two cholesterol measures in a clinical
trial, when the responses may be missing intermittently and follow-up may be
censored. \code{BiSCaL} implements the methods of Hu and Yi (2026). The treatment
\eqn{A} is conceptualized as two components, \eqn{A_1} acting on the first
response and \eqn{A_2} on the second (\eqn{A = a} corresponds to
\eqn{A_1 = A_2 = a}). Effects are defined at the end time point \eqn{K}
through generalized odds ratios (GOR) of the two responses: the total
treatment effect \eqn{TTE_K} compares \eqn{(A_1, A_2) = (1, 1)} with
\eqn{(0, 0)}, and the separable treatment effect \eqn{STE_{j,K}} changes
only the component acting on response \eqn{j}; they satisfy
\eqn{STE_{1,K} + STE_{2,K} = 2\,TTE_K}.
}
\section{Workflow}{

\enumerate{
\item Arrange the data in long format, one row per subject and visit, with the
visit index \verb{0, 1, ..., K}, the treatment and baseline covariates; code
missing responses as \code{NA} (see \link{simtrial} for an example).
\item Fit the transition and baseline models with \code{\link[=biscal]{biscal()}}; inspect them
with \code{summary()} (\link{biscal-methods}).
\item Estimate and test the effects for given covariate values with \code{\link[=aste]{aste()}},
or averaged over a covariate distribution with \code{\link[=easte]{easte()}}; \code{\link[=gor]{gor()}} and
\code{\link[=cf_prob]{cf_prob()}} give the underlying generalized odds ratios and
counterfactual distributions. Plot effects over follow-up with
\code{\link[=plot.biscal_aste]{plot.biscal_aste()}}.
}

\code{\link[=simulate_biscal]{simulate_biscal()}}, \code{\link[=biscal_coefs]{biscal_coefs()}} and \code{\link[=biscal_truth]{biscal_truth()}} reproduce the
simulation designs of the paper. See \code{vignette("BiSCaL")} for a worked
analysis.
}

